\DOC find_witnesses

\TYPE {find_witnesses : prover list -> term list -> term -> instantiation}

\SYNOPSIS
Use provers to choose an instantiation of some variables in a condition.

\DESCRIBE
A call {find_witnesses prs vs tm}, where {vs} is a list of variables, tries
to find an instantiation for {vs} that makes {tm} plausibly provable. Any
existential quantifiers round {tm} are stripped off and their bound variables
are treated in the same way as {vs}, so that they can be `seen through'.
The conjuncts of the body that involve any of these variables are collected
into a conjunction {p}, and the provers in {prs} are tried in turn on {p}
existentially quantified over all the variables, until one of them returns a
theorem whose conclusion is an instance of {p} (as {satisfy_prover} does). The
corresponding instantiation of the variables {vs} is returned, while that of
the existentially bound variables is discarded. Proving the instance of {tm}
is left to the caller.

The simplifier uses this in two places. When applying a conditional rewrite
{|- c ==> l = r}, it is used to instantiate the variables in {c} that are not
fixed by matching {l} against the term being rewritten (such as the
codomain {r'} in {RING_HOMOMORPHISM_MUL}, which also occurs in {r}). And
{basic_prover} uses it to choose witnesses for existential conditions, as
generated by {mk_rewrites} for variables that occur only in the condition.

\FAILURE
Fails if none of the provers produces a suitable instance.

\EXAMPLE
{
  # let pr = augment satisfy_prover
      [ASSUME `(x:A) IN s`; ASSUME `(s:A->bool) SUBSET t`];;
  val pr : prover = Prover (<fun>, <fun>)

  # find_witnesses [pr] [`u:A->bool`] `(x:A) IN u /\ u SUBSET t /\ P x`;;
  val it : instantiation = ([], [(`s`, `u`)], [])
}

\SEEALSO
basic_prover, IMP_REWR_CONV, satisfy_prover, SIMP_CONV.

\ENDDOC
